    "innovation": "This paper uses high-resolution regional climate models to show that the Greenland Ice Sheet's contribution to 21st-century sea level rise is substantially greater under CMIP6 climate projections than under equivalent CMIP5 scenarios, driven by a stronger Arctic Amplification signal and associated feedbacks in the newer models.",
    "kg_extraction_triplets": [
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "MAR",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_SOURCE_COMPONENT",
            "target_entity_instance": "Soil Ice Vegetation Atmosphere Transfer module",
            "target_entity_type": "SourceComponent",
            "paragraph": "Within MAR, the snow and ice properties at the ice sheet-atmosphere interface are calculated in the Soil Ice Vegetation Atmosphere Transfer module38. This module calculates the main snowpack based on the snow module CROCUS57,58, but also handles the mass and energy exchange between the atmosphere (e.g. radiation, precipitation, temperature) and the bare-ice surfaces, the snowpack, and the Arctic tundra that surrounds the GrIS38,40",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Soil Ice Vegetation Atmosphere Transfer module",
            "source_entity_type": "SourceComponent",
            "relation": "HAS_SOURCE_COMPONENT",
            "target_entity_instance": "CROCUS",
            "target_entity_type": "SourceComponent",
            "paragraph": "Within MAR, the snow and ice properties at the ice sheet-atmosphere interface are calculated in the Soil Ice Vegetation Atmosphere Transfer module38. This module calculates the main snowpack based on the snow module CROCUS57,58, but also handles the mass and energy exchange between the atmosphere (e.g. radiation, precipitation, temperature) and the bare-ice surfaces, the snowpack, and the Arctic tundra that surrounds the GrIS38,40",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "MAR",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_PHYSICAL_FEATURE",
            "target_entity_instance": "hydrostatic dynamical core",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalFeature",
            "paragraph": "MAR consists of a hydrostatic dynamical core which solves the primitive equation set38,39.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "CESM2",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_METRIC",
            "target_entity_instance": "Equilibrium Climate Sensitivity",
            "target_entity_type": "Metric",
            "paragraph": "Initial results of the notably greater ECS in CESM2 highlights that more sophisticated cloud microphysics are the main contributor to the greater climate sensitivity30.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "CESM2",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_METRIC",
            "target_entity_instance": "climate sensitivity",
            "target_entity_type": "Metric",
            "paragraph": "However, first analysis suggests, despite more sophisticated physics in CMIP6, that in individual models (CESM2) of the CMIP6 ensemble the climate sensitivity might not be in-line with paleoclimate records (i.e., too high) 48.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "MAR",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "IS_OF_TYPE",
            "target_entity_instance": "RCM",
            "target_entity_type": "SourceType",
            "paragraph": "Using a regional climate model (RCM; Mod\u00e9le Atmosph\u00e9rique R\u00e9gional, MAR2,38\u201341)\u2014which explicitly models important polar processes such as the surface mass balance (SMB), snow properties, and radiative transfer-to downscale six CMIP5 RCP8.5 and five CMIP6 SSP585 GCM projections to a higher spatial resolution, we find a cumulative increase of twenty-first century GrIS melt by +28,500 Gt (+7.9 cm sea-level equivalent (SLE) until 2100) and on average a 22 days longer melt season in the CMIP6 simulations.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "HadGEM2-ES",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "IS_OF_TYPE",
            "target_entity_instance": "GCM",
            "target_entity_type": "SourceType",
            "paragraph": "We chose six CMIP5 GCMS (HadGEM2-ES, MIROC5, NorESM1-M, ACCESS1.3, CSIRO-Mk3-6-0, and IPSL-CM5A-MR-all rlilp1 ensemble members) and five CMIP6 GCMS (CESM2, CNRM-CM6-1, CNRM-ESM2-1, MRI-ESM2-0, and UKESM1-0-LL - rlilp1f1 ensemble members, but f2 for CNRM and UKESM) for our dynamical downscaling with MAR.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
